"""Vendored crate sources, as the archives Cargo and Bazel both already understand.

Every registry crate in //:Cargo.lock is checked in here as `<name>-<version>.crate`.
//.bazelrc points `--distdir` at this directory, and rules_rs asks for exactly that
basename, so a crate resolves from the checked-in copy instead of crates.io.

Why archives rather than an extracted `cargo vendor` tree: the extracted form of this
dependency set is 38,949 files across 745 directories, and any version bump rewrites
whole directories. The archive form is 746 files, one per crate, and a bump changes
exactly one of them.

The trade-off, stated plainly: you cannot `grep` a dependency's source in-tree any
more. Extract an archive when you need to read one.

Refresh with:

    bazel run //third_party/crate_mirror:sync

`--distdir` is a fallback rather than an enforcement -- Bazel silently downloads
anything missing here rather than failing -- so a stale mirror degrades to the old
behaviour instead of breaking the build.
"""

load("@rules_python//python:defs.bzl", "py_binary")

package(default_visibility = ["//visibility:public"])

py_binary(
    name = "sync",
    srcs = ["sync.py"],
    main = "sync.py",
    # Deliberately NOT a build action. It writes into the source tree via
    # BUILD_WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY, which only `bazel run` provides.
    tags = ["manual"],
)

# The archives themselves are not build inputs -- Bazel reaches them through
# --distdir, not through the dependency graph -- so nothing here globs them into a
# target. This filegroup exists only so the directory is addressable.
filegroup(
    name = "archives",
    srcs = glob(
        ["*.crate"],
        allow_empty = True,
    ),
)
